-- R2 — Port Scan Detection
-- MITRE ATT&CK: T1595 (Active Scanning)
-- Status: Verified as R2b CRE rule after DSM Editor training. The original
-- AQL implementation below remains useful for the AQL workflow, but it is no
-- longer the primary port-scan detection.
--
-- Current rule: R2b - True Port Scan Detection
-- Test stack: at least 5 events are seen with the same Source IP and
-- different Destination Port in 5 minutes
-- Scope: Log Source Type = Netgate pfSense
--
-- Initial issue: Destination Port remained 0 after installing the
-- NetgatePfSense DSM. A real pfSense filterlog payload was assigned to
-- the pfSense-Firewall Log Source through DSM Editor, after which the
-- Destination Port field parsed correctly (53, 8080, 3389, 443, etc.).
--
-- Simulated traffic: a full TCP connect loop (netcat) against 7
-- distinct ports, deliberately NOT an nmap SYN scan, because pfSense
-- (a stateful firewall) handles unfinished SYN handshakes differently
-- from completed connections — using netcat better reflects the
-- connection behaviour a real brute-force tool like Hydra produces.
--
--   for port in 21 23 25 80 443 3389 8080; do
--       nc -zv -w 2 192.168.10.20 $port
--   done
--
-- Result observed: 10 one-minute windows exceeding the threshold,
-- peaking at 91 events in a single minute.

SELECT
    sourceip AS SourceIP,
    DATEFORMAT(starttime, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm') AS EventMinute,
    COUNT(*) AS EventCount
FROM events
WHERE sourceip = '192.168.10.20'
GROUP BY sourceip, EventMinute
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 5
LAST 1 HOURS
